1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,400
They're the quickest race cars on earth.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,140
Dragsters, whether professional models or souped up streetcars,

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:14,160
are machines designed purely for the thrill of acceleration.

4
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:19,740
The quest to build a car that goes just a bit faster than the next guy

5
00:00:19,741 --> 00:00:22,140
is one that's been going on for a century.

6
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,200
And it's a quest that's fraught with danger.

7
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,640
This is the story of that search.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,820
We'll take an inside look at what makes a top pro dragster run and get a glimpse of

9
00:00:35,821 --> 00:00:40,460
drag racing's technology of the future and we'll explore the current boom in

10
00:00:40,461 --> 00:00:44,980
Street Dragon, legal and otherwise.

11
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:53,480
Buckle up and join us for a ride at the edge. Drag racing, next on Cruise Control.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:14,000
It's Sunday morning at the California Speedway in Fontana.

13
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,480
Every weekend at strips like this around the country,

14
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:26,780
owners come to push their highly modified streetcars

15
00:01:26,781 --> 00:01:28,860
to the limit over a quarter mile.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
This is drag racing at its roots.

17
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
While pro racing is for the select few,

18
00:01:48,001 --> 00:01:51,000
there are tens of thousands of amateur draggers

19
00:01:51,001 --> 00:01:53,000
who devote most of their spare time and money

20
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:55,000
to the pursuit of speed.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
America has had a long love affair with hot rodding,

22
00:02:03,001 --> 00:02:06,000
and lately it's become a booming sport,

23
00:02:06,001 --> 00:02:09,000
with new drag strips opening across the country.

24
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
On this day, 500 drivers have each paid $20

25
00:02:17,001 --> 00:02:20,000
to make a guaranteed four runs.

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Any car that's street legal

27
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:27,000
and can pass the safety inspections can run.

28
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:29,000
No two cars are alike.

29
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:33,000
Each one has been customized inside and out to give it an edge.

30
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
This is a 78 Pontiac Trans Am.

31
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:44,000
Rudy Lopez has spent $25,000 on it so far.

32
00:02:44,001 --> 00:02:47,000
I took the engine out and I put this one in there.

33
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:50,000
It's a 455, the biggest motor Pontiac offered.

34
00:02:50,001 --> 00:02:53,000
I played with it, put some headers, put it in exhaust, intake.

35
00:02:53,001 --> 00:02:55,000
When you do horsepower, then you've got to upgrade everything else.

36
00:02:55,001 --> 00:02:56,100
then you got to upgrade everything else.

37
00:02:56,101 --> 00:03:00,080
So I redid the suspension, lower V-beer springs,

38
00:03:00,081 --> 00:03:02,600
gas shocks, tires.

39
00:03:02,601 --> 00:03:04,180
Most basically, I made it into a road racer.

40
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:14,460
Dillwyn Brando has reworked his 66 GTO

41
00:03:14,461 --> 00:03:17,880
to run at 500 horsepower, about 50% more

42
00:03:17,881 --> 00:03:19,960
than the factory version.

43
00:03:19,961 --> 00:03:21,460
The heads that are on here, I've

44
00:03:21,461 --> 00:03:23,800
had a little bit of port work done.

45
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:26,740
We put an Edelbrock Performer RPM manifold on.

46
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:30,300
I've got a Demon 850 carburetor under this air cleaner.

47
00:03:30,301 --> 00:03:32,500
We put the cannon air filter on here,

48
00:03:32,501 --> 00:03:34,980
and with that new top so that it gets more air

49
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:37,320
into the engine.

50
00:03:37,321 --> 00:03:41,160
The changes made his GTO one of the fastest cars

51
00:03:41,161 --> 00:03:45,460
at this meet, running the quarter mile in 11.4 seconds

52
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:52,560
from a standing start, hitting 123 miles per hour

53
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:53,560
by the finish line.

54
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,120
Amateur drag racers are now spending more than $1 billion

55
00:04:01,121 --> 00:04:02,940
a year on performance parts.

56
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:08,100
And increasingly, the cars of choice

57
00:04:08,101 --> 00:04:10,840
are not the traditional American muscle cars,

58
00:04:10,841 --> 00:04:13,440
but smaller, cheaper Japanese imports

59
00:04:13,441 --> 00:04:15,620
the racers call Rice Rockets.

60
00:04:15,621 --> 00:04:20,620
Well, this is a 1995 Honda Accord LX.

61
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:24,620
Got transformed from original engine, single cam 2.2,

62
00:04:24,621 --> 00:04:27,620
to a praline engine 2.2.

63
00:04:27,621 --> 00:04:32,620
So the different horsepower is from 165 stock to 220 horsepower.

64
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:34,620
I got the headers done.

65
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:35,620
I got the intake done.

66
00:04:35,621 --> 00:04:39,620
When I got this car with the stock engine that I had,

67
00:04:39,621 --> 00:04:42,620
when I ran it here, I hit a 16.5.

68
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:45,120
With this engine, it held me about two seconds.

69
00:04:45,121 --> 00:04:47,960
I'm hitting a 14.4.

70
00:04:47,961 --> 00:04:51,260
Japanese cars are cheap to buy secondhand.

71
00:04:51,261 --> 00:04:53,960
Performance upgrades are easy to find,

72
00:04:53,961 --> 00:04:56,460
and they allow room for creativity.

73
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:03,180
Why a Japanese car?

74
00:05:03,181 --> 00:05:05,680
Well, it's a lot funner to fix up a Japanese car

75
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:08,020
because the muscle car already has the power.

76
00:05:08,021 --> 00:05:10,620
You can buy it off the lot, and it'll be really fast.

77
00:05:10,621 --> 00:05:11,980
fast.

78
00:05:11,981 --> 00:05:13,560
But this one, it makes it a lot more funner,

79
00:05:13,561 --> 00:05:16,000
but it takes a whole lot to make it go as fast as a V8.

80
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,940
My name is Nalani, and I actually own this 1990 Honda

81
00:05:28,941 --> 00:05:30,600
CRX White.

82
00:05:30,601 --> 00:05:33,680
I've been racing for about a year and a half, maybe two,

83
00:05:33,681 --> 00:05:34,680
somewhere in there.

84
00:05:34,681 --> 00:05:37,940
A couple modifications to the car, DC headers, type R

85
00:05:37,941 --> 00:05:41,280
intake manifold, AM intake, just a stock motor,

86
00:05:41,281 --> 00:05:45,380
but runs pretty well, 14 fours in the quarter mile.

87
00:05:45,381 --> 00:05:46,940
It's male-dominant world out here,

88
00:05:46,941 --> 00:05:50,060
so it's a little intimidating, but once you get behind the wheel,

89
00:05:50,061 --> 00:05:50,761
it's all you.

90
00:05:50,762 --> 00:05:53,560
It's all about you.

91
00:05:53,561 --> 00:05:55,420
I just like it because I'm behind the wheel.

92
00:05:55,421 --> 00:05:57,200
I'm in charge of what's going on with the car,

93
00:05:57,201 --> 00:05:59,020
and it just gives you an adrenaline rush when you go

94
00:05:59,021 --> 00:06:00,260
through the gears and stuff.

95
00:06:00,261 --> 00:06:14,261
The growth in organized drag racing is a response to a recent rise in illegal street racing.

96
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:22,420
Police across the country have been waging campaigns against a trend so strong that it

97
00:06:22,421 --> 00:06:27,020
spawned the number one movie in 2001, The Fast and the Furious.

98
00:06:27,021 --> 00:06:32,020
Well, the top speed that I've ran so far on the freeway would be 140.

99
00:06:32,021 --> 00:06:34,020
That's not what I've ran so far.

100
00:06:34,021 --> 00:06:38,020
Well, maybe I shouldn't say that on TV, but too late now.

101
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:45,020
No one's pretending that legal drag meets have eradicated street racing,

102
00:06:45,021 --> 00:06:49,020
but officials say they are making a difference.

103
00:06:49,021 --> 00:06:53,020
You know, you talk to some of the kids that when you walk through the pits,

104
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:56,020
if they weren't here, they'd be out doing it on the street.

105
00:06:56,021 --> 00:06:58,520
I mean, people have a fascination with their cars.

106
00:06:58,521 --> 00:07:00,860
They love speed.

107
00:07:00,861 --> 00:07:03,520
We just like to get them in a controlled racing environment.

108
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,560
I tried to race it just here, because it's

109
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:13,380
getting too dangerous out there.

110
00:07:13,381 --> 00:07:15,980
Too many police, too many people.

111
00:07:15,981 --> 00:07:17,480
It's just getting too reckless.

112
00:07:17,481 --> 00:07:18,181
Please.

113
00:07:25,580 --> 00:07:28,500
The pull between legal and illegal drag racing

114
00:07:28,501 --> 00:07:29,860
is nothing new.

115
00:07:29,861 --> 00:07:34,060
It's a story nearly as old as the American automobile.

116
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:36,900
Coming up, the birth of the hot rod

117
00:07:36,901 --> 00:07:40,940
and how these modified factory cars morphed into dragsters.

118
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:58,920
When you look at the frightening power of a modern pro dragster, it's hard to believe

119
00:07:58,921 --> 00:08:05,200
that it's a direct descendant of the Model T Ford, but it is.

120
00:08:05,201 --> 00:08:11,480
No sooner did Henry Ford begin rolling out Model Ts from the assembly line in 1914, than

121
00:08:11,481 --> 00:08:17,040
young drivers began modifying them to see how fast they could go.

122
00:08:17,041 --> 00:08:21,920
In today's import racers, the Model Ts were relatively cheap,

123
00:08:21,921 --> 00:08:24,180
and spare parts were plentiful.

124
00:08:24,181 --> 00:08:28,480
From the 1920s on, street racers stripped their cars down

125
00:08:28,481 --> 00:08:30,420
to cut wind resistance.

126
00:08:30,421 --> 00:08:33,860
Then they added specialty parts, like 16 valve heads

127
00:08:33,861 --> 00:08:37,780
and tubular exhaust headers that helped to double or triple

128
00:08:37,781 --> 00:08:41,560
the Model Ts modest 20 horsepower engine.

129
00:08:41,561 --> 00:08:44,700
The cars were known as Hot Roadsters,

130
00:08:44,701 --> 00:08:45,800
Hot Rods for short.

131
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:52,300
In the 20s and 30s, owners raced their hot rods

132
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:54,560
on the dry lakes north of Los Angeles

133
00:08:54,561 --> 00:08:57,840
and hit speeds of 120 miles an hour.

134
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:01,900
Don Montgomery has spent a lifetime racing

135
00:09:01,901 --> 00:09:05,220
and writing about the early hot rods and dragsters.

136
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:08,840
This is an excellent example of a 1930s hot rod.

137
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:11,340
It was actually built in 1939.

138
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:13,540
It has a T body.

139
00:09:13,541 --> 00:09:17,480
The intake manifold, an Edelbrock manifold for three carburetors.

140
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:24,640
The maxi heads utilized the regular intakes, but moved the exhaust up into the head.

141
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:27,780
This car was clocked at 120.

142
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:37,720
Hot rod drivers also raced on public streets and became the target of newspapers and newsreels that called them dangerous, wild young men.

143
00:09:37,721 --> 00:09:41,560
Drag racers challenged each other to short sprints.

144
00:09:41,561 --> 00:09:44,060
A quarter mile became the accepted distance.

145
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:55,480
Amid official complaints, drag racing went legitimate.

146
00:09:55,481 --> 00:09:59,480
In 1949, at Goleta Airport in California,

147
00:09:59,481 --> 00:10:03,280
hot rod drivers held races with police approval.

148
00:10:03,281 --> 00:10:06,920
Soon, there were weekly drag races at Santa Ana

149
00:10:06,921 --> 00:10:10,440
at Santa Ana, California.

150
00:10:10,441 --> 00:10:14,300
And within two years, there were 130 sanctioned drag

151
00:10:14,301 --> 00:10:17,500
strips around the country.

152
00:10:17,501 --> 00:10:21,040
Many of them were supervised by the newly formed National Hot

153
00:10:21,041 --> 00:10:26,780
Rod Association, which still runs drag racing today.

154
00:10:26,781 --> 00:10:29,560
At first, the drivers raced the same cars

155
00:10:29,561 --> 00:10:32,700
they'd been using on the street or at the dry lakes,

156
00:10:32,701 --> 00:10:36,360
modified roadsters that doubled as their everyday transport.

157
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:45,360
Future Hall of Fame drag racer Tommy Ivo began his career

158
00:10:45,361 --> 00:10:47,060
with this altered Model A.

159
00:10:49,580 --> 00:10:51,580
It was my very first hot rod.

160
00:10:51,581 --> 00:10:53,940
I built it when I was 19 years old,

161
00:10:53,941 --> 00:10:56,200
picked up a body that we found out there,

162
00:10:56,201 --> 00:10:59,660
put it on a Model A frame, and took a Buick engine

163
00:10:59,661 --> 00:11:01,780
and put it in, a Buick V8.

164
00:11:01,781 --> 00:11:05,800
And then, of course, the headers that come out the side, the big wings there.

165
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:09,240
The less restriction you have, the better it is.

166
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:11,840
We don't want it running through mufflers and everything,

167
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:16,680
so we have a little cap that we take off the end that would let it just run out the back.

168
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:19,780
And, of course, it'd make an enormous amount of noise,

169
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:22,540
and, of course, the more noise you make, the better it is.

170
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:29,500
This particular car right here, the best, it'd run 119 miles an hour and a quarter mile.

171
00:11:29,501 --> 00:11:34,280
And I'm telling you, we thought we were just ripping the asphalt off the ground to do that.

172
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:39,880
Drivers soon began sacrificing looks for performance.

173
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:46,440
To improve the acceleration they needed to win drag races, they stripped their cars mercilessly.

174
00:11:47,140 --> 00:11:53,320
This 1950 example, called the Bug, reached 103 miles an hour over the quarter mile.

175
00:11:53,321 --> 00:12:00,240
It took the racers probably 10 minutes to realize that the lighter car was the winner.

176
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:03,160
So this is an example of what was built.

177
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:09,260
This car was actually put together with a roadster body and everything not needed thrown away.

178
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:10,960
There's no radiator on it.

179
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:15,300
The only important thing in the whole car, as far as most of the racers are concerned, was the engine.

180
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:18,700
The rest of it was just something to get them there.

181
00:12:18,701 --> 00:12:22,740
This car is what leads into the dragsters of the future.

182
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:26,320
Dragster design changed quickly in the 50s,

183
00:12:26,321 --> 00:12:29,320
a time of intense experimentation.

184
00:12:29,321 --> 00:12:31,320
Cars became narrower and lighter,

185
00:12:31,321 --> 00:12:34,320
with longer wheelbases aimed at shifting the weight

186
00:12:34,321 --> 00:12:36,320
to the rear tires for traction.

187
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:41,320
Legendary driver Art Christman was the first man

188
00:12:41,321 --> 00:12:45,320
to hit 140 miles an hour at the drag strip.

189
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:50,320
He ran 9.4 seconds for the quarter in this modified roadster

190
00:12:50,321 --> 00:12:53,320
roadster that he still keeps in tune today.

191
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:01,320
When I got it, like I say, it was a very short wheelbase car,

192
00:13:01,321 --> 00:13:03,320
and it didn't handle very good.

193
00:13:03,321 --> 00:13:05,320
You know, it was pretty squirrely.

194
00:13:05,321 --> 00:13:08,320
So we made it longer and set the driver further back,

195
00:13:08,321 --> 00:13:12,320
and it just straightened it right out.

196
00:13:12,321 --> 00:13:14,320
Mark Crispin, next.

197
00:13:14,321 --> 00:13:19,320
145 miles an hour, the fastest single engine time of the meet.

198
00:13:19,321 --> 00:13:27,440
Art boosted the power of his Ford V8 by running four carburetors for better fuel supply.

199
00:13:27,441 --> 00:13:30,260
But speeds were limited by the tires.

200
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:35,380
50s technology could not make them big enough to give the needed traction.

201
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:39,640
We run 140 miles an hour with tires that are five inches wide, you know.

202
00:13:39,641 --> 00:13:43,360
And now they're, you know, three times that wide.

203
00:13:43,361 --> 00:13:46,320
In their search for speed, 50s drag racers

204
00:13:46,321 --> 00:13:50,360
experimented with a rocket fuel called nitromethane,

205
00:13:50,361 --> 00:13:54,960
a fuel so effective it's still used today.

206
00:13:54,961 --> 00:13:58,160
Nitromethane needs only 1 10th as much oxygen

207
00:13:58,161 --> 00:14:02,460
as gasoline does to ignite, so race cars can use much more

208
00:14:02,461 --> 00:14:06,460
of it and create more power.

209
00:14:06,461 --> 00:14:09,360
We run nitromethane, which back in those days,

210
00:14:09,361 --> 00:14:12,960
we were on straight nitro to get all the power

211
00:14:12,961 --> 00:14:14,500
could, you know.

212
00:14:14,501 --> 00:14:17,000
The more fuel you put in it, the faster it ran

213
00:14:17,001 --> 00:14:22,460
until it didn't like it anymore, and then it blew the crank out.

214
00:14:22,461 --> 00:14:26,200
Using nitro, dragsters hit unthought of speeds

215
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:29,300
around 170 miles an hour.

216
00:14:29,301 --> 00:14:32,220
Officials thought that was too fast to be safe.

217
00:14:32,221 --> 00:14:38,160
So in 1957, the National Hot Rod Association banned nitro.

218
00:14:38,161 --> 00:14:42,620
Dragsters would have to use pump gasoline.

219
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:45,420
Drivers looked for new power sources,

220
00:14:45,421 --> 00:14:49,460
like multiple engines.

221
00:14:49,461 --> 00:14:53,800
Tommy Ivo built this twin-engine dragster in 1959

222
00:14:53,801 --> 00:14:56,340
and became the first man to run the quarter

223
00:14:56,341 --> 00:15:02,180
in less than nine seconds on gasoline.

224
00:15:02,181 --> 00:15:03,780
Good grief.

225
00:15:03,781 --> 00:15:06,980
I ran 180 miles an hour in this thing.

226
00:15:06,981 --> 00:15:10,360
I guess it proves when you're younger, you're fearless, huh?

227
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:14,660
Now this was really unusual.

228
00:15:14,661 --> 00:15:18,080
As you can see, the engines are tilted.

229
00:15:18,081 --> 00:15:20,360
There's only one drive shaft in the car.

230
00:15:20,361 --> 00:15:23,000
So we transferred the horsepower from that motor

231
00:15:23,001 --> 00:15:25,740
to that motor through the ring gears

232
00:15:25,741 --> 00:15:29,100
and then used one clutch to run it out the back.

233
00:15:29,101 --> 00:15:33,420
So we had to rig this motor up so it ran backwards

234
00:15:33,421 --> 00:15:36,200
and the other one ran forward like it was supposed to

235
00:15:36,201 --> 00:15:39,460
so it could mesh the ring gears together.

236
00:15:39,461 --> 00:15:43,000
Two engines worked so well that in 1960,

237
00:15:43,001 --> 00:15:46,340
Tommy Ivo decided to try four of them.

238
00:15:46,341 --> 00:15:48,920
But the extra weight made the car a little too heavy

239
00:15:48,921 --> 00:15:50,340
to run records.

240
00:15:50,341 --> 00:15:53,300
Also, because the car was four-wheel drive,

241
00:15:53,301 --> 00:15:57,380
it needed heavy racing tires on both back and front.

242
00:15:57,381 --> 00:16:02,100
That meant extra traction and extra smoke.

243
00:16:02,101 --> 00:16:03,380
You'd step on the gas.

244
00:16:03,381 --> 00:16:05,380
The front tires would start smoking.

245
00:16:05,381 --> 00:16:08,800
It'd come rolling back across the car.

246
00:16:08,801 --> 00:16:12,780
And it was odd because, oh, just for about 50, 100 feet,

247
00:16:12,860 --> 00:16:14,640
you couldn't see quite where you was going.

248
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:17,680
So there was a lot of looking until the wind would probably

249
00:16:17,681 --> 00:16:21,140
carry the smoke away so that you could see where you were going.

250
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:29,820
The late 50s and early 60s was drag racing's golden age

251
00:16:29,821 --> 00:16:30,620
of experimentation.

252
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:34,660
But it often came at a terrible price.

253
00:17:03,814 --> 00:17:07,500
DRAG RACING DESIGNERS IN THE LATE 50'S AND EARLY 60'S

254
00:17:07,501 --> 00:17:09,040
tried almost everything.

255
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:16,380
Multiple engines,

256
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:21,260
rocket fuel,

257
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:26,140
even aircraft engines,

258
00:17:26,440 --> 00:17:27,940
although they proved too heavy.

259
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:32,300
There was also the question

260
00:17:32,301 --> 00:17:33,860
of where to put the driver.

261
00:17:34,900 --> 00:17:37,300
The favorite place was over the rear axle.

262
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:40,500
to get weight onto the back tires.

263
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:48,500
This design was called the slingshot.

264
00:17:48,501 --> 00:17:50,500
The main thing is getting the traction,

265
00:17:50,501 --> 00:17:52,500
and you didn't have the traction until you started

266
00:17:52,501 --> 00:17:54,500
sitting behind the rear end.

267
00:17:54,501 --> 00:17:56,500
You sit behind the rear end, sit lower,

268
00:17:56,501 --> 00:17:58,500
you got a lot more traction.

269
00:17:58,501 --> 00:18:01,500
Then Tommy Imo makes a spectacular run,

270
00:18:01,501 --> 00:18:04,500
looking very much like a rocket shot up the Cape.

271
00:18:04,501 --> 00:18:06,500
Time, 185 miles an hour.

272
00:18:06,501 --> 00:18:07,960
an hour.

273
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:11,200
You just kind of got used to the slingshot idea

274
00:18:11,201 --> 00:18:14,540
of having your legs draped across the rear end here.

275
00:18:14,541 --> 00:18:16,040
You'd have the throttle, of course,

276
00:18:16,041 --> 00:18:17,740
that you would push to open.

277
00:18:17,741 --> 00:18:19,820
You'd have the clutch to work.

278
00:18:19,821 --> 00:18:22,580
Of course, the parachute, you'd push that out first.

279
00:18:22,581 --> 00:18:25,040
And then your hand would just move over to the brake

280
00:18:25,041 --> 00:18:26,620
and pull it on down.

281
00:18:26,621 --> 00:18:30,360
Because things happen so fast when you're running 180 miles

282
00:18:30,361 --> 00:18:33,240
an hour and you've got a quarter of a mile to stop

283
00:18:33,241 --> 00:18:37,680
that you didn't make any wasted motions.

284
00:18:37,681 --> 00:18:40,800
The slingshot experiment proved dangerous.

285
00:18:40,801 --> 00:18:43,680
With the driver sitting directly behind the increasingly

286
00:18:43,681 --> 00:18:45,900
volatile engine, it meant engines

287
00:18:45,901 --> 00:18:49,640
could and did blow up literally in the driver's face.

288
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:57,600
Also, experiments with lighter bodies and greater horsepower

289
00:18:57,601 --> 00:19:01,020
caused many cars to flip out, and dozens of drivers

290
00:19:01,021 --> 00:19:03,220
were killed.

291
00:19:03,221 --> 00:19:06,860
I think there was a lot of cars out there that were way too light.

292
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:10,540
There's a lot of guys that built lightweight cars that aren't around anymore.

293
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:16,520
And I always had a heavy car, and I never did crash one.

294
00:19:19,020 --> 00:19:22,660
After nitromethane was allowed again in 1963,

295
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:27,180
the shape of the modern dragster started to become standardized.

296
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:31,340
Drivers needed only one engine on nitro,

297
00:19:31,341 --> 00:19:34,000
so dragsters became leaner and lighter.

298
00:19:34,001 --> 00:19:35,840
Most were still slingshots.

299
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:46,880
With that combination in 1964, Don Garlitz

300
00:19:46,881 --> 00:19:50,560
became the first man to officially clock 200 miles

301
00:19:50,561 --> 00:19:57,900
per hour in this car.

302
00:19:57,901 --> 00:20:00,700
This car here was powered by a supercharged Chrysler

303
00:20:00,701 --> 00:20:05,369
Chrysler engine. It developed at that time approximately 1,500 horsepower.

304
00:20:05,370 --> 00:20:06,070
By today's

305
00:20:06,071 --> 00:20:07,704
standards, very simple technology.

306
00:20:07,705 --> 00:20:10,360
This is the same basic engine you would have probably

307
00:20:10,361 --> 00:20:15,080
had in your passenger car if you had had a Chrysler Imperial.

308
00:20:15,081 --> 00:20:16,860
It's a Chrysler V8, 392

309
00:20:16,861 --> 00:20:19,010
cubic inches, as produced from the factory.

310
00:20:19,011 --> 00:20:21,260
And at this time, they used most of the basic

311
00:20:21,261 --> 00:20:24,133
components of the original Chrysler engine.

312
00:20:24,134 --> 00:20:27,540
The big change was the addition of the supercharger

313
00:20:27,541 --> 00:20:30,340
in place of the carburetors that would have been on a stock car.

314
00:20:35,140 --> 00:20:37,920
But just as the dragster was being refined,

315
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:40,720
a whole new kind of drag car was emerging.

316
00:20:41,520 --> 00:20:45,760
In the mid-60s, designers began taking Detroit stock cars

317
00:20:45,761 --> 00:20:48,220
and moving the wheels forward about a foot

318
00:20:48,221 --> 00:20:50,080
to push weight to the back.

319
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:53,740
They inched the rear wheels forward a few inches,

320
00:20:53,900 --> 00:20:55,680
then the front wheels forward a few inches.

321
00:20:55,681 --> 00:21:00,680
pretty soon someone looked at them and says that really looks funny and the name funny car came about.

322
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:13,680
This car is a little 62 Chevy 2 which was basically an economy car.

323
00:21:13,681 --> 00:21:20,680
Initially they came with four cylinders and six cylinders and the back wheel was originally back in this area

324
00:21:20,681 --> 00:21:29,680
and shoved it forward, took the front wheels, just like you see here, which were originally back in this area,

325
00:21:29,681 --> 00:21:34,680
shoved it forward, which gave the weight transfer, got all the traction on the back.

326
00:21:34,681 --> 00:21:38,680
And as you see, it's no longer capable of keeping the engine under the hood.

327
00:21:38,681 --> 00:21:44,680
You have the Hillborne injector stacks on a big block Chevrolet, and what had been an economy car

328
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:46,980
was now capable of running the quarter mile

329
00:21:46,981 --> 00:21:50,380
at over 150 miles an hour in just a little more than nine

330
00:21:50,381 --> 00:21:51,081
seconds.

331
00:21:57,220 --> 00:22:00,020
Funny cars were a huge hit with fans

332
00:22:00,021 --> 00:22:04,440
because they looked like their own street cars.

333
00:22:04,441 --> 00:22:07,640
But under the hood, funny cars had enough power

334
00:22:07,641 --> 00:22:11,340
to challenge the fastest dragsters.

335
00:22:11,341 --> 00:22:14,280
This is a 1971 Dodge Challenger.

336
00:22:14,281 --> 00:22:17,280
When it's up, you see the sophisticated race car.

337
00:22:17,281 --> 00:22:19,280
They have the fuel tank up in the nose,

338
00:22:19,281 --> 00:22:23,280
the nitromethane alcohol mixture goes into the fuel pump,

339
00:22:23,281 --> 00:22:28,280
then up to the fuel injector hat at the top of the supercharger.

340
00:22:28,281 --> 00:22:33,280
And this is all mounted on an iron block 426 Chrysler.

341
00:22:33,281 --> 00:22:40,280
This car was capable of probably 215 to 220 miles an hour in the quarter,

342
00:22:40,281 --> 00:22:43,280
which is obviously a great progression from the little Chevy 2

343
00:22:43,281 --> 00:22:46,980
that was barely over 150 miles an hour.

344
00:22:46,981 --> 00:22:49,280
Although racing in separate classes,

345
00:22:49,281 --> 00:22:52,380
funny cars began clocking times nearly as fast

346
00:22:52,381 --> 00:22:56,320
as open dragsters.

347
00:22:56,321 --> 00:23:02,180
The dragster design is lighter, and it gets off the line quicker.

348
00:23:02,181 --> 00:23:05,980
But the funny car's aerodynamic shell and extra downforce

349
00:23:05,981 --> 00:23:07,280
almost make up the difference.

350
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:24,960
The open dragster remained king of the track and advances in horsepower, tires and body

351
00:23:24,961 --> 00:23:28,040
design push speeds ever higher.

352
00:23:28,041 --> 00:23:35,140
They broke the 250 mile per hour barrier in 1975, covering the quarter mile in five and

353
00:23:35,141 --> 00:23:37,560
a half seconds.

354
00:23:37,561 --> 00:23:40,800
The 300 barrier fell in 1992.

355
00:23:40,801 --> 00:23:48,280
What we have here is the first drag racing vehicle to exceed 300 miles an hour in the quarter mile.

356
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:52,500
Virtually everything in the car has just been stepped up a notch.

357
00:23:53,140 --> 00:23:55,400
This supercharger right here is much larger.

358
00:23:55,560 --> 00:23:58,800
This is basically what they call a 1471 supercharger.

359
00:23:59,560 --> 00:24:04,420
Two huge magnetos to keep this fuel mixture lit.

360
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:09,420
Everything has just gotten bigger and stronger, and it's just a game of more.

361
00:24:09,421 --> 00:24:14,220
Today, dragsters can hit 330 miles per hour,

362
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:18,860
eating up the quarter mile in 4 and 1 half seconds.

363
00:24:18,960 --> 00:24:22,360
Wheelbases have stretched to 300 inches.

364
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:25,160
Giant rear wings have added downforce,

365
00:24:25,260 --> 00:24:28,280
and engines have been moved behind the driver,

366
00:24:28,380 --> 00:24:30,680
improving traction and safety.

367
00:24:33,980 --> 00:24:37,080
Funny cars have kept pace along the way,

368
00:24:37,081 --> 00:24:41,320
using similar engine advances plus aerodynamic improvements

369
00:24:41,420 --> 00:24:45,020
to consistently run above 320 miles an hour.

370
00:24:49,520 --> 00:24:52,600
Coming up next, one of today's top drivers

371
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:55,760
takes us step by step through the technology

372
00:24:55,860 --> 00:24:58,340
that makes up a state of the art dragster.

373
00:24:58,341 --> 00:25:08,340
The job of a drag race driver is straightforward.

374
00:25:08,341 --> 00:25:12,340
To go from zero to more than 300 miles an hour

375
00:25:12,341 --> 00:25:17,340
in just over four seconds, it's a dangerous task

376
00:25:17,341 --> 00:25:21,340
that's claimed scores of racers over the years.

377
00:25:21,341 --> 00:25:23,340
You better be paid up and prayed up.

378
00:25:23,341 --> 00:25:26,340
You're just really on the edge.

379
00:25:26,341 --> 00:25:29,540
all your attention and all your energy is focused

380
00:25:29,541 --> 00:25:32,580
on the starting line.

381
00:25:32,581 --> 00:25:34,140
If you're late on the starting line,

382
00:25:34,141 --> 00:25:37,080
you'll lose every time.

383
00:25:37,081 --> 00:25:41,180
Gary Clapshaw is one of the top drivers in America today.

384
00:25:41,181 --> 00:25:44,420
He has to pilot a machine that mounts an engine

385
00:25:44,421 --> 00:25:48,100
as powerful as a freight train on top of a thin web

386
00:25:48,101 --> 00:25:51,040
of metal alloy tubing.

387
00:25:51,041 --> 00:25:54,340
You have to find the strength from within

388
00:25:54,341 --> 00:25:57,440
within to match the strength of the car.

389
00:25:57,441 --> 00:26:00,180
Otherwise, the car will overpower you,

390
00:26:00,181 --> 00:26:03,720
and you'll end up either not making it down the track

391
00:26:03,721 --> 00:26:06,520
or worse.

392
00:26:06,521 --> 00:26:10,160
Clapshaw's success, and indeed his very life,

393
00:26:10,161 --> 00:26:14,400
depend on a crew of experts who test, tune, strip,

394
00:26:14,401 --> 00:26:19,580
and rebuild his car after every single run.

395
00:26:19,581 --> 00:26:23,160
Clapshaw took us inside his Las Vegas workshop

396
00:26:23,161 --> 00:26:25,600
for a detailed look at a state of the art,

397
00:26:25,601 --> 00:26:29,460
top fuel dragster, beginning with the tremendous engine.

398
00:26:30,740 --> 00:26:35,100
The average automobile may have 350 horsepower.

399
00:26:35,101 --> 00:26:36,640
These have 7,000.

400
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:42,040
But the design of the motor is an American V8,

401
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:45,940
just like what has been built and put in regular cars

402
00:26:45,941 --> 00:26:48,060
for the last 30, 40 years.

403
00:26:48,061 --> 00:26:49,960
But this is an aftermarket design.

404
00:26:49,961 --> 00:26:51,120
It's all aluminum.

405
00:26:51,121 --> 00:26:54,160
is there's only one or two engine makers,

406
00:26:54,161 --> 00:26:57,160
motor builders that produce these blocks.

407
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:03,160
Helping to drive the engine are an injector

408
00:27:03,161 --> 00:27:05,160
and a supercharger.

409
00:27:05,161 --> 00:27:09,160
Together, they force massive amounts of air into the engine,

410
00:27:09,161 --> 00:27:12,160
mixing with the fuel to create a stronger combustion.

411
00:27:12,161 --> 00:27:14,160
This is the injector.

412
00:27:14,161 --> 00:27:17,160
The supercharger sits right underneath it.

413
00:27:17,161 --> 00:27:19,160
As the air goes through the injector,

414
00:27:19,161 --> 00:27:21,860
and then it's compressed with the supercharger.

415
00:27:22,360 --> 00:27:26,700
And that's how she breathes, and that's what makes the fire.

416
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:33,740
One reason dragsters generate such awesome power

417
00:27:33,741 --> 00:27:35,980
is their fuel, nitromethane.

418
00:27:36,340 --> 00:27:39,740
It's burning nitro that you see coming out of the exhaust system.

419
00:27:43,880 --> 00:27:48,120
Once banned as too powerful, nitro is still restricted.

420
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:52,400
Race fuel can be no more than 90% nitro,

421
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:55,180
so it's mixed with less powerful alcohol.

422
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:02,000
They had some serious explosions a few years ago on 100%,

423
00:28:02,001 --> 00:28:05,600
so they cut it back to try and limit blower explosions.

424
00:28:07,360 --> 00:28:09,760
Dragsters burn an incredible amount of fuel

425
00:28:09,761 --> 00:28:13,060
at the rate of over 150 gallons a minute.

426
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:17,740
The 32 fuel injection nozzles would empty a streetcar's gas tank

427
00:28:17,741 --> 00:28:20,540
in about 10 seconds.

428
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:22,140
These are the fuel pump levers.

429
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:23,840
You have two pumps on the car.

430
00:28:23,940 --> 00:28:27,640
One is the low side, the other is the high side.

431
00:28:27,740 --> 00:28:31,480
And just before you leave, you turn both pumps on.

432
00:28:31,580 --> 00:28:36,000
You start the car with one and do your burnout with one pump.

433
00:28:36,100 --> 00:28:38,200
And as you pull up into the lights,

434
00:28:38,300 --> 00:28:39,500
you put the other one on.

435
00:28:39,501 --> 00:28:51,940
This model of a single cylinder shows the huge fuel appetite.

436
00:28:51,941 --> 00:28:55,400
This is how much fuel one cylinder uses when idling.

437
00:28:55,401 --> 00:28:58,260
But look at what happens when the race driver floors the pedal.

438
00:29:03,460 --> 00:29:05,880
The motors are extreme.

439
00:29:05,881 --> 00:29:08,600
And on nitromethane, there's nothing else

440
00:29:08,601 --> 00:29:11,100
As a driver, if you're not careful,

441
00:29:11,101 --> 00:29:13,400
you can go out and you can explode these motors one

442
00:29:13,401 --> 00:29:14,840
right after another.

443
00:29:14,841 --> 00:29:17,100
And here are the fragments of what

444
00:29:17,101 --> 00:29:21,140
was once a motor before nitromethane exploded

445
00:29:21,141 --> 00:29:23,840
inside the engine block.

446
00:29:23,841 --> 00:29:25,360
It split the engine.

447
00:29:25,361 --> 00:29:28,360
I've just got a small chunk of the engine here.

448
00:29:28,361 --> 00:29:31,260
You can see where the head gasket's there.

449
00:29:31,261 --> 00:29:33,360
This is the rear section of the block.

450
00:29:33,361 --> 00:29:35,960
There would be two more cylinders up here

451
00:29:35,961 --> 00:29:39,460
Two more cylinders up here and two more cylinders over here.

452
00:29:39,461 --> 00:29:43,260
So this is a third of the engine, I guess.

453
00:29:43,261 --> 00:29:44,960
This here is a supercharger.

454
00:29:44,961 --> 00:29:46,660
Split the side off.

455
00:29:46,661 --> 00:29:48,960
Everything that there was to break, it broke.

456
00:29:48,961 --> 00:29:54,460
It would be approaching $50,000.

457
00:29:54,461 --> 00:29:57,060
To try to avoid such catastrophes,

458
00:29:57,061 --> 00:29:59,860
the crew checks the car after every run.

459
00:29:59,861 --> 00:30:03,360
On race days, they have to be able to strip and reassemble

460
00:30:03,361 --> 00:30:05,860
it in about an hour.

461
00:30:05,861 --> 00:30:07,820
One part that gets special attention

462
00:30:07,821 --> 00:30:11,420
is the clutch assembly, which channels the engine's horsepower

463
00:30:11,421 --> 00:30:13,140
to the tires.

464
00:30:13,141 --> 00:30:16,900
The clutch's interlocking plates are put under such pressure

465
00:30:16,901 --> 00:30:21,920
that they last for only one or two runs.

466
00:30:21,921 --> 00:30:24,720
They just disintegrate, basically.

467
00:30:24,721 --> 00:30:26,500
They disintegrate, and they get so hot,

468
00:30:26,501 --> 00:30:29,860
and the plates in between, it all welds together

469
00:30:29,861 --> 00:30:31,120
and sticks together.

470
00:30:31,121 --> 00:30:34,020
So these plates in between are replaced every time.

471
00:30:34,021 --> 00:30:40,580
This is a good comparison between a brand new and used pitted glazed over disk tears them up.

472
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:50,900
So this is the computer power system that all the information is gathered into and then is stored in here.

473
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:59,780
And at the end of the run, we open this up and we put the chip on it and we draw everything out of the system.

474
00:30:59,781 --> 00:31:01,740
and we can take it into the trailer

475
00:31:01,741 --> 00:31:03,820
and put it on the computer screen,

476
00:31:03,940 --> 00:31:06,420
and it'll tell us exactly what took place.

477
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:10,420
To handle the enormous horsepower,

478
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,720
dragsters need huge tires for traction.

479
00:31:14,480 --> 00:31:16,680
Each of the rear tires, called slicks,

480
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:18,700
is 17 inches wide.

481
00:31:19,680 --> 00:31:22,560
These tires are smooth, as you can see,

482
00:31:23,020 --> 00:31:25,400
and in normal conditions,

483
00:31:25,540 --> 00:31:26,800
these would have no traction

484
00:31:26,801 --> 00:31:29,400
on a regular street application.

485
00:31:29,401 --> 00:31:31,860
However, when you go to the racetrack,

486
00:31:31,861 --> 00:31:35,400
and the track has been prepared, and it is very sticky

487
00:31:35,401 --> 00:31:39,240
on the surface, and when you have a very sticky surface

488
00:31:39,241 --> 00:31:44,840
on the track, that allows for a complete surface coverage

489
00:31:44,841 --> 00:31:47,560
of the tire on the track.

490
00:31:47,561 --> 00:31:51,660
And it's like a bonding of an adhesive.

491
00:31:51,661 --> 00:31:55,760
High above the rear tires, modern dragsters have a wing.

492
00:31:55,761 --> 00:31:57,460
It's not there for lift.

493
00:31:57,461 --> 00:31:59,360
Quite the opposite.

494
00:31:59,361 --> 00:32:04,360
The rear wing is designed to create downforce on the back tires.

495
00:32:04,361 --> 00:32:08,360
So as you start down the track, and as you gain mile an hour,

496
00:32:08,361 --> 00:32:14,360
the pressure increases to a point to where you have over 5,000 pounds

497
00:32:14,361 --> 00:32:17,360
of downforce on the rear tires.

498
00:32:17,361 --> 00:32:21,360
If you didn't have this, the tires would spin with this much horsepower.

499
00:32:21,361 --> 00:32:23,360
You could never get them hooked up.

500
00:32:23,361 --> 00:32:28,360
And when those tires hook up, the metal tubing chassis of a modern dragster

501
00:32:28,361 --> 00:32:35,460
dragster bends under extreme stress under acceleration the chassis is made to flex

